  "big_story": {
    "headline": "RBI Bulletin: Growth Amid Market Stress",
    "subhead": "GDP TOUCHES 7.8% AS FOREX RESERVES DROP AND EQUITIES SLIP FOR A SEVENTH WEEK",
    "paragraphs": [
      "The Reserve Bank of India's latest bulletin highlights a resilient Indian economy registering 7.8% GDP growth despite persistent global headwinds and West Asia conflict risks. However, foreign exchange reserves plummeted by $14.88 billion in a single week to $765.90 billion for the week ended September 18. Meanwhile, Indian equity indices registered a seventh straight weekly loss, marking the longest Nifty losing streak since the Covid pandemic.",
      "The market divergence is striking as RBI Deputy Governor Poonam Gupta noted that the bond market performed well while equities faced continuous selling pressure. Rupee gains backed by foreign currency non-resident deposits reversed in September due to elevated crude oil prices. Global trade tensions, inflation concerns, and geopolitical friction in West Asia continue to present downside risks to domestic financial stability.",
      "Looking ahead, central bank officials remain optimistic that equities will become attractive again as domestic macroeconomic fundamentals stay robust. Investors are closely monitoring crude oil price movements and global interest rate bets that impact foreign capital flows. The coming weeks will reveal if bond market strength can translate into broader financial market stabilization."
    ],
    "pullbox": "While India maintains a solid 7.8% GDP growth rate, a $14.88 billion drop in forex reserves and a seven-week equity downturn highlight severe global headwinds.",
    "image": {
      "url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Seal_of_the_Reserve_Bank_of_India.svg/330px-Seal_of_the_Reserve_Bank_of_India.svg.png",
      "alt": "Reserve Bank of India"
    }
  },
  "explains": {
    "title": "What are Foreign Exchange Reserves?",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Foreign exchange reserves are foreign assets held by a central bank, such as the Reserve Bank of India, in foreign currencies. These reserves include foreign banknotes, treasury bills, and government securities used to meet international financial obligations.",
      "In practice, strong reserves allow a nation to absorb global economic shocks and support its domestic currency during foreign exchange volatility. When import costs rise or foreign capital exits, central banks draw on these reserves to maintain exchange rate stability."
    ]
  },
  "venture_vault": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Hospitality AI startup Dextr AI has raised $6.7 million in a seed funding round led by Elevation Capital. The company builds automated AI agents specifically tailored for hotels to manage guest operations and digital workflows.",
      "The fresh capital will be deployed to expand its specialized AI agents across international hotel chains and upgrade technology infrastructure. The funding comes as Indian tech startups raised $10.3 billion in 2026, with vertical AI applications drawing larger investor cheques."
    ]
  },
